<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Anglo-Maratha War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1803">1803</a>–<a href="/years/1805">1805</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> War was fought between the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and the Maratha chiefs Scindia of Gwalior, Bhonsle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> and later Holkar of Indore. British armies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Arthur Wellesley</a> and Gerard Lake won at Assaye, Laswari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1803. The Marathas lost large territories, including Delhi and the Mughal emperor's custody, and the Company became the leading power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The British had supported the Peshwa Raghunathrao in the First Anglo-Maratha War and continued their support for his son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-ii">Baji Rao II</a>. He provoked the enmity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yashwant-rao-holkar">Yashwant Rao Holkar</a> by having one of Holkar's relatives killed. The Maratha Empire was then a confederacy of five major chiefs: the Peshwa at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Poona</a>, the Gaekwad of Baroda, the Scindia of Gwalior, the Holkar of Indore and the Bhonsle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a>, who quarrelled among themselves. Lord Mornington, the Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, had repeatedly offered a subsidiary treaty to the Peshwa and Scindia, but Nana Fadnavis strongly refused.</p><p>In October 1802 the combined armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-ii">Baji Rao II</a> and Scindia were defeated by Yashwantrao Holkar at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Poona</a>. Baji Rao fled to British protection and in December concluded the Treaty of Bassein with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, ceding territory to maintain a subsidiary force and agreeing to make treaties with no other power. The treaty has been called the death knell of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Scindia and Bhonsle rulers contested the agreement. The British planned for Major General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Arthur Wellesley</a> to secure the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a> and for Lt. Gen. Gerard Lake to take the Doab and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, with other columns sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand">Bundelkhand</a>, Badoch and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, and over 53,000 men available. Wellesley attacked on 8 August 1803, taking the pettah of Ahmednagar the same day, and the fort surrendered on 12 August. In September 1803 Scindia forces lost to Lake at Delhi and to Wellesley at Assaye. British forces took the pettah of Asirgarh on 18 October and its garrison surrendered on the 21st. In November Lake defeated another Scindia force at Laswari, and Wellesley defeated Bhonsle forces at Argaon on 29 November 1803.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>On 17 December 1803 Raghoji II Bhonsale signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-deogaon">Treaty of Deogaon</a> and gave up the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuttack">Cuttack</a>. On 30 December Daulat Scindia signed the Treaty of Surji-Anjangaon, ceding Hisar, Panipat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohtak">Rohtak</a>, Rewari, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurugram">Gurgaon</a>, the Ganges-Jamuna Doab, the Delhi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> region, parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand">Bundelkhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Broach</a>, some districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a>.</p><p>The British began hostilities against Yashwantrao Holkar on 6 April 1804. He had some success by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare, but received no help from Scindia, who had already signed with the British, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> refused it. Lack of resources forced him to terms, and the Treaty of Rajghat of 24 December 1805 made him give up Tonk, Rampura and Bundi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Anglo-Maratha War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Maratha embassy under the Vakil of the Peshwa also sat at the court until the <b>Second Anglo-Maratha War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/awadh-state">Awadh State · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Abdul Samad Khan, a Pathan soldier, served Lord Lake in the <b>Second Anglo-Maratha War</b>, and in 1806 the British granted him Dujana and its villages as a hereditary state.</blockquote><a href="/state/dujana-state">Dujana State</a></li><li><blockquote>Daulat Rao Scindia lost the <b>Second Anglo-Maratha War</b> in 1803 and moved his military camp, Lashkar, to Gwalior.</blockquote><a href="/state/gwalior-state">Gwalior State</a></li></ul></section>
